hief medical officer made the point  last night that there is no perfect  time to act and secondarily uh the  the advice that we were receiving at the  time was that it was perfectly  reasonable to take a regional approach  and we weren't  outliers in that regard that was the  position that the french government  the german government and that others  were taking  um we've taken the steps that we have  now uh because we recognized that the  the rate of increase of the virus as i  mentioned earlier and as you  acknowledged  has been greater than was anticipated  and there are lessons that we've learned  from the first  lockdown lessons that we've learned  about how we treat people in hospital  which means that people are less likely  to die  lessons about testing which meant that  we've increased our testing capacity  to uh nearly 500 000 and rising  and also one of the other lessons that  we've learned uh from then  is the vital importance of making sure  that uh we protect the nhs  and also do everything that we can at  the appropriate moment  to support the economy which is also why  the chancellor of the exchequer  announced yesterday the extension of the  furthest one of the major lessons of the  first lockdown  the major mistakes was not protecting  people in care homes some of the most  vulnerable people  and yet once again it feels like you're  making the same mistakes my colleague ed  conway revealed this week that in leeds  for example  covert 19 is now higher in care homes  than at the first  peak of the virus there and the  government guidance still says that  people  who've tested positive can be discharged  from hospitals into care homes no well i  i  i say several things about um the care  home sector the first thing is  that uh we are uh making sure that  the spread of the virus in care hymns um  with the benefit of  the analysis that we've done has often  been linked to people who've been  working in more than one care home  um and what we've done is we've put in  place protocols infection control  protocols  also people been discharged from  hospitals into care the same thing is  happening let me make three points the  first is  that um as we look back the evidence  suggests that the single greatest uh  cause of the spread of the virus in care  homes was from people moving between  them  we have infection control protocols in  order to prevent that secondly  we've provided hundreds of thousands of  pounds to care homes in order to ensure  uh that their staff can effectively  isolate and  staff are being tested as well weekly in  the care home sector  the third thing is that when it comes to  anyone who's being discharged  we're making sure that anyone who is  discharged from  a hospital into a care home is in a  secure and isolated setting  and the care quality commission is  looking at  the increasing capacity of those  specific beds  which ensure that anyone who is  discharged is in a  safe environment so all of this is in  the winter plan for care  that my colleague helen waitley has  published and it is a  detailed plan in order to ensure that we  mitigate and reduce the risk of  infection okay now um you mentioned the  economic support  earlier will that economic support be  extended  to cover the entire period for which  these restrictions  last for well the economic support is  going to extend  for the period until december the 4th  but what we've always said  is that we review the support that we  are giving  uh the chancellor has said that we are  there to put our hands  around individuals and the economies  well again i don't want to anticipate  the decisions that will be taken between  now and december the 4th  as you quite rightly point out with a  virus that's malignant  and with its capacity to move so quickly  um it would be foolish to predict with  absolute certainty what will happen  in four weeks time when over the course  of the last two weeks  its rate its infectiousness um uh its  malignancy  have grown and so therefore of course  we'll review what requires to be done  but we have a clear plan over the course  of the next four weeks to support the  economy  and to protect the nhs why was it  impossible to pay  80 of workers wages in greater  manchester  when two weeks later you did it for the  whole country well one of the things  about that time is that we were seeking  to negotiate with the mayor of  manchester and others in order to ensure  that we could move  from tier two to tier three and the  mayor at the time and  others were arguing that the uh uh that  tier two was where they wanted to stay  even though the economic support  they wanted just one second even though  the economic support they would have got  would have been greater  in tier three and in tier three they  would have received both more economic  support  and also they would have done more to  contribute to restricting  uh the spread of the budget i didn't  agree to 80 of wages  we did agree to a generous economic  package which would have been uh  my question which would have been  welcome had it been introduced earlier  why is it  the the lesson from 80 of workers wages  in greater manchester but okay to pay  the whole country  eight percent awareness now well the two  things the first thing i would say is  that  uh the delay in greater manchester in  moving towards tier three  has led to an increase in infections the  the evidence shows that  and then the third thing is that we were  talking to local leaders about what was  required  in circumstances where the restrictions  were uh  uh less than than they will be now  because the the restrictions that we're  putting in place across the united  kingdom  are stricter uh for understandable  reasons and reasons that we've discussed  than the restrictions that would have  been in place in greater manchester  at that time just finally and there's an  inquiry  into who revealed details of the  lockdown on friday  of course we know who was in the meeting  was the chancellor the prime minister  and the house secretary should the  person who leaked the details keep their  job  well there's a leak inquiry going on and  it's for the prime minister and the  cabinet secretary  to decide what action needs to be taken  but what do you think  i think it's for the prime minister and  the cabinet secretary to decide uh that  investigation i think is very important  uh because it's absolutely vital that  when we have discussions  uh serious discussions uh that it's  possible for  myself and for other ministers and more  critically for our advisors  to feel that they can share information  in confidence i think that's very  important
 

